WEBVTT

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:14.237
<v><b>Jorge:</b></v> I think Oprah Winfrey is a really inspirational person,

00:00:15.357 --> 00:00:17.924
because of her resilience and empathy.

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:22.999
I know she had a tough childhood marked by poverty and hardship.

00:00:23.522 --> 00:00:26.894
She faced abuse and significant struggles growing up.

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:32.871
But despite all of that, she managed to build an incredibly successful career in media,

00:00:33.586 --> 00:00:37.814
starting as a news anchor and eventually creating her own talk show,

00:00:38.561 --> 00:00:43.533
which became a global phenomenon, and ran it for 25 years.

00:00:44.207 --> 00:00:50.069
Um, what I admire most is her ability to connect with people on a deep level.

00:00:50.669 --> 00:00:58.183
She seems to genuinely care about others and uses her platform to inspire and empower millions.

00:00:58.581 --> 00:01:03.189
Even after achieving so much, she continues to advocate for education,

00:01:03.214 --> 00:01:05.381
 mental health, and equality.

00:01:06.013 --> 00:01:10.721
Oprah stands out because she doesn’t just focus on her own success.

00:01:11.036 --> 00:01:15.022
She uses her influence to make a positive impact on society.

00:01:20.696 --> 00:01:24.000
<v><b>Beatriz:</b></v> So the person I’ve chosen as the person,

00:01:24.025 --> 00:01:29.671
one of the people I most admire is the pop icon, George Michael.

00:01:30.126 --> 00:01:33.597
He was, in my view, he was just like,

00:01:33.703 --> 00:01:37.274
he was so important to me in my formative years,

00:01:37.591 --> 00:01:43.163
you know, when you’re sort of 14, 15, 16, um, because of his music.

00:01:43.236 --> 00:01:48.103
You may not have heard of him, but in the 1980s, he was a huge,

00:01:48.128 --> 00:01:52.674
he had a huge impact on a whole generation of us.

00:01:53.039 --> 00:01:58.061
One of the things that does, I do remember is that, you know, he was born in 1963,

00:01:58.086 --> 00:02:01.947
which is the same year I was born in and he came from an immigrant background.

00:02:01.956 --> 00:02:07.046
I think it was Greek Cypriot, and his origins were quite humble.

00:02:07.989 --> 00:02:11.247
On a pers…you know, as regards his personal qualities,

00:02:11.642 --> 00:02:18.700
he always came across as being friendly and creative and mega-unassuming.

00:02:19.136 --> 00:02:23.092
Um, and all of this, despite him being an icon.

00:02:24.048 --> 00:02:28.500
I think he…well, I mean, I know from what he said in several interviews,

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:33.626
he had great difficulties, um, connected to his sexuality.

00:02:34.115 --> 00:02:39.089
And to my mind, he really stands out because in a day when many of us

00:02:39.665 --> 00:02:44.622
or many people are bragging about what they do for charities,

00:02:44.647 --> 00:02:53.385
what they do for people in need, and telling everyone about it, it wasn’t until he died, in 2016,

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:57.952
I think, that it came to light that this person, George Michael,

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:05.655
had done so much for charities, helped so many people in need, but without making it public.

00:03:13.702 --> 00:03:19.190
<v><b>Panoraia:</b></v> I think Greta Thunberg is a really inspirational figure.

00:03:19.812 --> 00:03:25.853
And, that is because to me, she represents that one voice can make a difference.

00:03:25.878 --> 00:03:30.777
And it doesn’t really matter how young you are, inexperienced, um,

00:03:30.802 --> 00:03:36.391
if you don’t have powerful friends, money, um, or power yourself.

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:42.985
So, um, she started in Sweden, er, when she was 15 years old. She started activism.

00:03:43.317 --> 00:03:47.480
She started protesting, um, about climate change.

00:03:47.776 --> 00:03:54.718
Now, even though in the beginning, her example was, um, mocked by politicians and she received,

00:03:54.753 --> 00:04:01.967
er, severe criticism, gradually, students from all over the world followed her example

00:04:01.992 --> 00:04:07.955
and her paradigm and started protesting outside their, um, their parliaments as well.

00:04:08.316 --> 00:04:14.000
So I find her highly inspirational for her courage, for her determination,

00:04:14.481 --> 00:04:21.941
even though she has also claimed that she has, um, a kind of autism, she has Asperger’s,

00:04:22.404 --> 00:04:28.103
and this is what actually, er, gives her strength or determination or focus on

00:04:28.128 --> 00:04:34.314
what she does because she doesn’t get distracted by other ‘social games’ as she calls them.

00:04:34.699 --> 00:04:40.117
However, I do believe she’s a role model for young people,

00:04:40.142 --> 00:04:42.529
and I think that young people are the future.